<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hafez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1325–1390</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Hafiz of Shiraz, Khwaja Shams al-Din Muhammad Hafez</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hafez was a Persian poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, the most celebrated master of the ghazal, or lyric love poem. He lived under the Injuid and Muzaffarid rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, praising some patrons and mocking the piety of others. His collected poems, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divan">Divan</a>, mix love, wine and mysticism, are still widely read and recited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and are used for divination.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hafez.jpg" alt="Hafez" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anselm Feuerbach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anselm_Feuerbach_Hafis_vor_der_Schenke_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultans also corresponded with foreign courts, exchanging embassies with the Ming emperors and, according to a well-known tradition, inviting the Persian poet <b>Hafez</b> to Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Shiraz remained an active centre of poetry, painting and calligraphy, and Karim Khan restored the tomb of <b>Hafez</b> in 1773, while the oil paintings and lacquerwork of the Zand court foreshadowed the Qajar court style that followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hafez</b> of Shiraz, who died around 1390, is regarded by many Iranians as the greatest of their poets, and copies of his poems are found in Iranian homes, where they are opened for guidance.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>On the longest night of the year friends and family gather to eat, drink and read poetry, especially <b>Hafez</b>, late into the night; pomegranates and watermelons are particularly significant.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu Ishaq Inju, who took power around 1343, was a cultivated and lavish ruler; Ibn Battuta visited his court, and <b>Hafez</b> and the satirist Ubayd Zakani praised him.</blockquote><a href="/state/injuid-dynasty">Injuid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Mubariz al-Din was a stern ruler who closed taverns in Shiraz, a mood <b>Hafez</b> mocked in his verse; in 1358 his sons blinded and deposed him.</blockquote><a href="/state/muzaffarid-dynasty">Muzaffarid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.61,52.53&amp;year=1367" aria-label="Shiraz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5917 1082 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hafez"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6367 1352h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6367 1352h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.61,52.53&amp;year=1367">What was here in 1367? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Arise, oh Cup-bearer, rise! and bring To lips that are thirsting the bowl they praise, For it seemed that love was an easy thing, But my feet have fallen on difficult ways.&#8221;</p><footer>Divan, ghazal 1 &#183; tr. Gertrude Bell, 1897 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74883/pg74883-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;From Canaan Joseph shall return, whose face A little time was hidden: weep no more— Oh, weep no more! in sorrow’s dwelling-place The roses yet shall spring from the bare floor!&#8221;</p><footer>Divan &#183; tr. Gertrude Bell, 1897 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74883/pg74883-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Where are the tidings of union? that I may arise— Forth from the dust I will rise up to welcome thee! My soul, like a homing bird, yearning for Paradise, Shall arise and soar, from the snares of the world set free.&#8221;</p><footer>Divan &#183; tr. Gertrude Bell, 1897 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74883/pg74883-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ah, seek the treasure of a mind at rest And store it in the treasury of Ease; Not worth a loyal heart, a tranquil breast, Were all the riches of thy lands and seas!&#8221;</p><footer>Divan &#183; tr. Gertrude Bell, 1897 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74883/pg74883-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the Conqueror’s reward Is never worth the army’s long-drawn woes, Worth fire and sword.&#8221;</p><footer>Divan &#183; tr. Gertrude Bell, 1897 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74883/pg74883-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The rose is not fair without the beloved’s face, Nor merry the Spring without the sweet laughter of wine…&#8221;</p><footer>Divan &#183; tr. Gertrude Bell, 1897 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74883/pg74883-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
